+The original split assigned train, validation and test variants by STRING protein ID, with no shared proteins across partitions. The split contained 141,864 training variants, 17,129 validation variants and 18,220 test variants. The homology-aware split used MMseqs protein clusters with minimum sequence identity 0.40, coverage 0.50 and coverage mode 0 \cite{Steinegger2017MMseqs2}. Each cluster was assigned wholly to one partition by greedy balancing of variant-row counts, without label-prevalence stratification, preserving the same row counts. We use this split as a stress test. Its test positive rate was 10.56\%, compared with 31.95\% in the original split, which limits direct cross-split AUPRC comparison.
+
+All current comparisons used downstream MLPs with 512/128 hidden units, dropout 0.3 and validation-AUC checkpoint selection. Training used class-weighted binary cross-entropy and Adam with learning rate $10^{-3}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$ and batch size 1,024 \cite{Kingma2015Adam}. The positive-class weight was the training negative-to-positive count ratio. Classifier seeds 42--44, a maximum of 50 epochs and patience five were shared across arms; permutation controls crossed three graph permutations with the same classifier seeds. CADD v1.7 and AlphaMissense scores were matched to variant records \cite{Schubach2024CADDv17,Cheng2023AlphaMissense}. Missing values were imputed using training-set medians, with an AlphaMissense missingness indicator; inputs were not standardized. Models shared hidden layers and preprocessing, although score-only models had fewer input weights. Training hyperparameters, score coverage and historical protocols are detailed in Supplementary Methods 3 and 5 and Tables 1 and 8.
 
 \subsection{External-source and second-network validation}
 
-Frozen original-split raw-ESM, STRING-FPP and BioGRID-FPP classifiers were evaluated on UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot HUMSAVAR 2026\_03 without external fitting or tuning \cite{UniProt2025}. Canonical sequences had to match a unique frozen graph node exactly, with the reference residue verified. We excluded original variants using the union of accession-, node- and gene-plus-substitution keys and dbSNP identifiers. The primary set excluded original training/validation proteins and identical full-length sequences: 10,785 variants from 2,498 proteins. A nested sensitivity set also excluded original test proteins and identical sequences: 6,934 variants from 1,713 proteins. All remained nodes of the frozen transductive graph; these exclusions do not establish patient, evidence or full homology independence.
+We evaluated frozen classifiers trained on the original split using raw ESM, STRING FPP or BioGRID FPP. External evaluation used HUMSAVAR 2026\_03 from UniProtKB/\allowbreak{}Swiss-Prot, with no external fitting or tuning \cite{UniProt2025}. Pathogenic/likely pathogenic records were positive and benign/likely benign records negative; uncertain-significance records were excluded. Canonical sequences had to match a unique frozen graph node exactly, with the reference residue verified. We excluded original variants using the union of accession-, node- and gene-plus-substitution keys and dbSNP identifiers. The primary set excluded original training/validation proteins and identical full-length sequences: 10,785 variants from 2,498 proteins. A nested sensitivity set also excluded original test proteins and identical sequences: 6,934 variants from 1,713 proteins. All remained nodes of the frozen transductive graph; these exclusions do not establish patient, evidence or full homology independence.
 
 BioGRID 5.0.262 human physical-category records \cite{Stark2006BioGRID} were filtered to remove RNA-related methods and ambiguous mappings, then mapped to the same 19,622 nodes. The graph contained 931,458 unique undirected edges and 1,402 isolates; all test rows were retained. Nine additional classifiers covered BioGRID representation-only prediction and original/homology fusion, using the same operator, seeds and training budget. Of its edges, 56.54\% also occurred in STRING. Paired protein-bootstrap intervals used 2,000 resamples of three-seed mean probabilities, conditional on fitted models and without multiplicity adjustment. Full filtering, run verification and AUPRC results are in Supplementary Methods 6 and Tables 14--17.
 
